Yes. Here is the exact backtesting flow we are implementing.

1. 5-minute candles decide the strategy

Your main strategy still runs on 5-minute candles.

For every completed 5m candle we calculate the EMA and determine its direction.

For a BUY signal:

EMA trend = UP

Pattern A:
Open < EMA
Close > EMA

OR

Pattern B:
Open > EMA
Close > EMA
Low <= EMA

For a SELL signal:

EMA trend = DOWN

Pattern A:
Open > EMA
Close < EMA

OR

Pattern B:
Open < EMA
Close < EMA
High >= EMA

Nothing about the signal-generation strategy was changed.

2. Signal candle becomes T0

Suppose a BUY signal forms:

T0 5m candle

High = 105  ← BUY trigger
Low  = 98   ← Stop Loss

We cannot enter on T0 itself.

The pending setup becomes:

BUY above 105
SL = 98
3. Entry Valid For Next Candles controls how long we wait

Suppose:

Entry Valid For Next Candles = 2

Then:

T0 = Signal candle       ❌ cannot enter
T1 = First valid candle  ✅
T2 = Second valid candle ✅
T3 = Expired             ❌

So 1-minute processing does NOT mean the setup stays valid longer.

4. A 5m breakout is only the first check

Suppose T1 has:

Open = 103
High = 108
Low  = 97
Close = 106

Because:

108 > BUY trigger 105

the 5m candle tells us that the trigger was crossed.

But it cannot tell us when 105 was crossed relative to the movement toward 98.

Therefore we inspect T1's 1-minute candles.

5. Find the actual entry using 1m candles

Imagine T1 consists of:

1m #1: High 104
1m #2: High 104.8
1m #3: High 106  ← first high > 105
1m #4
1m #5

Entry occurs on:

1m #3
Entry price = 105

For SELL, it is the opposite:

1m low < SELL trigger
6. Your special rule for the entry minute

This is particularly important.

Suppose BUY:

Trigger = 105
SL = 98

And the entry 1m candle is:

High = 106
Low  = 97

That same minute contains both:

Entry trigger crossed
AND
SL crossed

With OHLC data, we don't know whether price went:

105 → 106 → 98

or:

98 → 105 → 106

Your chosen rule is:

TAKE THE ENTRY.

Do NOT check SL or TP
on the entry 1-minute candle.

Therefore the trade is not stopped out on that minute.

7. Protection begins from the NEXT 1m candle

Suppose entry happened at:

10:02

Then:

10:02 → entry
        SL/TP ignored

10:03 → SL/TP active
10:04 → SL/TP active
...

This applies even though 10:03 and 10:04 are still inside the same 5m candle.

8. Normal later candles are inexpensive to process

Suppose a later 5m candle has:

SL = 98
TP = 126

If its range is:

Low = 101
High = 120

neither is reached → continue.

If:

Low = 97
High = 120

only SL is reached → SL.

If:

Low = 101
High = 127

only TP is reached → TP.

We don't need 1m analysis for those cases because there is no ambiguity.

9. If BOTH SL and TP occur in a later 5m candle

This is the part we corrected in the latest version.

Suppose:

SL = 98
TP = 126

5m:
Low  = 97
High = 128

Both were touched somewhere within those five minutes.

We do not automatically choose SL.

Instead, inspect the 1m candles chronologically.

Example:

10:15  100–110
10:16  103–120
10:17  105–127  ← TP
10:18   97–108  ← SL
10:19

TP occurred first.

Result:

TP ✅

If instead:

10:15  100–110
10:16   97–108  ← SL
10:17  105–127  ← TP

Result:

SL ❌
10. What if the SAME later 1m candle contains SL and TP?

Example:

SL = 98
TP = 126

1m:
Low  = 97
High = 128

Even 1m OHLC cannot tell which happened first.

So we use the agreed conservative rule:

Both inside same 1m
        ↓
       SL
11. What if 5m says breakout but 1m doesn't confirm it?

We do not invent the entry.

For example:

T1 5m high = 106
Trigger = 105

but available T1 1m data
never shows high > 105

No confirmed entry is created from T1.

And importantly, if:

Entry Valid = 2

T2 is still allowed.

So:

T0 → signal

T1 → 5m breakout
     but no reliable 1m confirmation
     → NO fabricated trade
     → setup remains pending

T2 → valid 5m + 1m breakout
     → ENTER

T3 → too late
12. Optimizer uses exactly the same execution logic

The optimizer isn't supposed to use a simplified version of the strategy.

Every parameter combination goes through the same:

5m signal
    ↓
Entry-valid window
    ↓
5m breakout
    ↓
1m entry confirmation
    ↓
Ignore entry-minute SL/TP
    ↓
Protection from next minute
    ↓
Later 5m SL/TP
    ↓
If both → inspect 1m
13. Performance improvement

We don't rebuild millions of 1m candles for every optimizer trial.

Instead:

Fetch 1m candles
       ↓
Build 1m lookup ONCE
       ↓
Trial 1 ─┐
Trial 2  │
Trial 3  ├→ same lookup
...      │
Trial N ─┘

That was one of the major performance fixes.

